'''''Euphorbia polygona''''', the '''many-angled cactus euphorbia''', is a plant in the [[spurge family]] native to dry areas of South Africa.

==Description==
''Euphorbia polygona'' is a hairless succulent with many stems that can be 15&nbsp;centimeters to 2&nbsp;meters in length.{{sfn|Bruyns|2022|p=157}} The overall appearance is very similar to columnar cactuses from the Americas.{{sfn|Scott|1958|p=141}} Stems branch near the base of the plant, very rarely further up, and grow a fibrous root network from the base of the main stem or some of the branches. On occasion plants will produce short [[rhizome]]s, 2–10&nbsp;cm long. The stems tend to be club-shaped, larger in diameter towards the top than at the base, with a width of 4–30&nbsp;cm, though usually not more than 20&nbsp;cm.{{sfn|Bruyns|2022|p=157}}

The stems are covered in [[tubercles]], cone shaped outgrowths, they fuse to make seven to twenty ribs on the stems.{{sfn|Bruyns|2022|pp=157–158}} They are often quite narrow and wavy.{{sfn|Bruyns|2022|p=158}} The groves between the ribs can be as much as 1.5&nbsp;cm deep;{{sfn|Rauh|1984|p=49}} they can go straight up the stems or less frequently twist around them.{{sfn|Scott|1958|p=141}} Towards the top of the stems small 1–2&nbsp;mm long and 1&nbsp;mm wide vestigial leaves are produced, but are not retained for long.{{sfn|Bruyns|2022|p=157}}

The thorns on the ribs are long lasting [[Peduncle (botany)|peduncles]], the stalks that attach to the flowers,{{sfn|Bruyns|2012|p=517}} 0.4–4&nbsp;cm long and 1–3&nbsp;mm thick at the base.{{sfn|Bruyns|2022|p=157}} Some of these peduncles are modified to never produce flowers and on other plants they are absent.{{sfn|Bruyns|2022|p=157}} Usually the spines are in groups of two to five.{{sfn|WFO|2026}} The flowers are produced near the top of the stems, but not at its very end, in the joints between the tubercles atop the ribs.{{sfn|Bruyns|2022|p=157}}

==Taxonomy==
''Euphorbia polygona'' was given its scientific name in 1803 by [[Adrian Hardy Haworth]]. It is part of genus ''[[Euphorbia]]'' which is classified in the [[Euphorbiaceae]] family.{{sfn|POWO|2026}} According to [[Plants of the World Online]] it has no accepted [[Variety (botany)|varieties]],{{sfn|POWO|2026}} but due to many botanists reducing ''[[Euphorbia horrida]]'' to a [[Synonym (taxonomy)|synonym]] of ''E.&nbsp;polygona'' the botanist Detlef H. Schnabel has argued for the recognition of a variety named ''horrida'' to prevent the loss of genetic diversity in the species.{{sfn|Schnabel|2013|pp=6–7}}

If ''Euphorbia horrida'' is considered to be a synonym, then ''Euphorbia polygona'' has {{table row counter|id=Synonyms}} [[heterotypic synonym]]s including 4 species and 14 varieties.{{sfn|POWO|2026}}

